Transforming a Content Website Into a High-Performance Shopify Store

For Parfums de Marly, moving to Shopify was not simply a platform migration. It was part of a broader website optimization project that combined ecommerce development, redesign, SEO migration, and performance optimization.

The existing website was primarily focused on presenting content and product information rather than supporting a complete online purchasing journey. The project therefore required us to preserve the site's existing content and SEO value while introducing a full ecommerce structure.

At the same time, the new website needed a more modern interface, stronger internal connections between content and products, and better technical performance.

From the beginning, the project centered on:

migrating the existing WordPress website to Shopify;

transforming the content-driven site into a complete ecommerce storefront;

preserving important SEO pages and existing content;

creating a new Shopify design and page structure;

improving internal linking between content and commercial pages;

optimizing Shopify theme performance and Core Web Vitals;

validating the complete website after migration.

Migrating Existing Content to Shopify

One of the first challenges was moving the existing content without losing the structure and SEO value that had already been established.

We migrated the site's core:

pages;

articles;

informational materials;

images;

other required media assets.

The content was then adapted to the new Shopify structure rather than simply copied into a different CMS.

This required balancing two objectives: maintaining important existing content while restructuring the website around its new ecommerce functionality.

Important SEO pages and valuable content were retained as part of the migration, providing continuity between the original website and the new Shopify implementation.

This approach made the migration more than a basic WordPress to Shopify content transfer. It became a structured Shopify website migration designed around both content preservation and the new commercial architecture.

Building a Complete Ecommerce Structure

The biggest functional change was the transformation of the website from a primarily content-focused platform into a complete online store.

The new ecommerce structure allows users to move through the entire purchasing journey directly on the website:

product discovery → product selection → add to cart → checkout

We implemented:

a structured product catalog;

product browsing and selection;

product information presentation;

add-to-cart functionality;

shopping cart functionality;

the complete purchasing flow;

order checkout through Shopify Checkout.

This created a new direct sales channel while allowing the site's existing informational content to remain an important part of the customer journey.

Instead of separating content and commerce, we connected them so that users could move naturally from learning about the products to purchasing them.

Connecting Content With Commerce

One of the key UX goals was to make the existing content more commercially useful without turning the website into a purely transactional storefront.

The new structure creates stronger relationships between informational pages and the product catalog.

This means content can support product discovery, while product pages provide a direct path toward purchase.

The resulting journey is more cohesive:

users discover relevant information;

they can explore the associated products;

product pages provide a clear next step;

products can be added directly to the cart;

the purchase can be completed through Shopify Checkout.

This created a stronger content-to-commerce UX and made the existing content a more integrated part of the ecommerce experience.

Redesigning the Shopify Storefront

The migration also included a complete redesign of the website's visual structure.

Rather than reproducing the original interface, we created a new design adapted specifically to the requirements of a Shopify ecommerce environment.

The redesign included:

a new visual structure;

redesigned key pages;

updated content block layouts;

a more consistent interface;

mobile-oriented interface improvements;

layouts designed to work across both content and commercial pages.

The main challenge was creating a unified visual system that could accommodate two different types of content: informational pages and ecommerce pages.

The result was a more coherent Shopify custom storefront where content, products, navigation, and purchasing functionality follow the same overall design language.

Custom Shopify Development for Content and Ecommerce

The new design was implemented directly in Shopify and adapted to the platform's ecommerce capabilities.

The development work focused on creating a structure that could support both existing content and new commercial functionality.

This included:

custom Shopify page structures;

ecommerce-oriented layouts;

reusable content elements;

product-focused components;

responsive interface behavior;

integration between content pages and the shopping experience.

This approach created a more flexible Shopify custom development foundation instead of treating the redesign as a collection of isolated page templates.

The result is a storefront architecture capable of supporting both editorial content and product-driven journeys within the same ecommerce system.

SEO Migration Built Around Existing Search Value

SEO was one of the most important parts of the migration because changing platforms and restructuring a website can create significant search visibility risks if the transition is not carefully controlled.

Before the migration, we analyzed the existing site structure and identified important pages and content that needed to be preserved.

The SEO work included:

analyzing the existing website structure;

preserving important SEO pages;

retaining valuable existing content;

configuring URL redirects where the structure changed;

optimizing Title and Meta Description elements for key pages;

reviewing heading structure;

improving internal linking;

checking technical indexing after migration.

Particular attention was given to the relationship between informational and commercial pages.

By improving internal linking between these areas, we created a stronger Shopify internal linking structure and clearer paths for both users and search engines.

The result was a migration strategy designed to preserve existing SEO value while providing a stronger technical foundation for the new ecommerce website.

Optimizing Titles, Meta Descriptions, and Heading Structure

The migration also provided an opportunity to improve important on-page SEO elements.

For key pages, we reviewed and optimized:

Title tags;

Meta Descriptions;

heading hierarchy;

page structure;

internal links.

These elements were considered alongside the new Shopify page architecture rather than optimized independently.

The goal was to create pages that were easier for users to understand while also providing a cleaner information hierarchy for search engines.

This formed part of a broader Shopify SEO optimization process rather than a one-time metadata update.

Shopify Performance Optimization

Performance was another major focus of the project.

The migration was used as an opportunity to review the technical resources loaded by the storefront and reduce unnecessary performance overhead.

Our optimization work included:

improving page loading behavior;

reducing unnecessary resources;

optimizing images;

optimizing third-party script loading;

reviewing the Shopify theme;

checking key performance indicators;

validating Core Web Vitals.

Performance optimization is particularly important for an ecommerce website because the user experience depends on multiple layers loading efficiently — from content and images to product interfaces, cart functionality, and third-party services.

By addressing these areas as part of the migration, we created a cleaner technical foundation for the new Shopify storefront.

Optimizing the Shopify Theme and Third-Party Resources

A significant part of the performance work focused on the relationship between the Shopify theme and the resources loaded by the website.

We reviewed unnecessary resources and worked on improving how assets, images, and third-party scripts were loaded.

This helped reduce technical overhead and create a more efficient Shopify theme performance setup.

Image optimization was also part of the process, ensuring that visual content could retain its role in the redesigned storefront without creating unnecessary loading costs.

The result was a more performance-conscious Shopify implementation built around both visual quality and technical efficiency.

Post-Migration SEO and Technical QA

The migration did not end when the new Shopify storefront went live.

A dedicated post-migration QA process was used to validate the new website across its main technical, ecommerce, and SEO layers.

We checked:

key website pages;

links and redirects;

responsive behavior across devices;

product catalog functionality;

shopping cart functionality;

Shopify Checkout;

technical SEO elements;

indexing and page accessibility.

This final validation was important because migration issues can appear only after the new platform is fully assembled.

By testing the storefront after implementation, we were able to verify that the new ecommerce system, SEO structure, and redesigned interface were working together as intended.

Combining Shopify Migration, SEO, and Performance

What makes the Parfums de Marly project particularly valuable is the combination of several complex objectives within one migration.

The project was not approached as:

WordPress → Shopify

Instead, it was treated as:

WordPress → Shopify + ecommerce transformation + redesign + SEO migration + performance optimization

Each part of the project influenced the others.

The ecommerce structure affected the information architecture. The redesign affected how content and products were presented. The new URL structure required redirects. SEO required careful content preservation and internal linking. Performance optimization had to account for the new Shopify theme and ecommerce functionality.

This integrated approach allowed the migration to improve the website on multiple levels simultaneously.

The Result: A Faster, SEO-Ready Shopify Ecommerce Foundation

Parfums de Marly was successfully migrated from WordPress to Shopify with a complete ecommerce structure, redesigned interface, SEO optimization, and performance improvements.

The new website provides users with a complete commercial journey:

discover product information;

explore the product catalog;

select products;

add products to the cart;

complete purchases through Shopify Checkout.

At the same time, the project preserved important content and SEO pages, introduced a more structured SEO foundation, improved internal linking, and addressed key technical performance factors.

The final result is a more modern and commercially capable Shopify ecommerce website with:

WordPress to Shopify migration

Shopify ecommerce development

Shopify custom storefront development

Shopify SEO migration

Shopify redirects and URL preservation

Shopify internal linking optimization

Shopify theme performance optimization

image and third-party script optimization

Core Web Vitals optimization

post-migration technical QA

For Crocode, Parfums de Marly is a strong example of Shopify migration combined with SEO and performance optimization — demonstrating how a platform change can become an opportunity to improve the entire ecommerce infrastructure rather than simply reproduce an existing website on a new CMS.

Transforming a Content Website Into a Full Shopify Store

Urban Sign Print needed more than a platform migration. The existing website was primarily a content-focused WordPress site and did not provide a complete ecommerce experience where customers could browse products, add them to a cart, and place orders directly online.

The project became an opportunity to solve several challenges at once: migrate the existing website from WordPress to Shopify, introduce a complete online sales system, and completely redesign the storefront and user experience.

Instead of simply reproducing the existing website on a new platform, we transformed it into a full Shopify ecommerce store with a product catalog, shopping cart, and Shopify Checkout.

From the beginning, the project centered on:

migrating the existing WordPress website to Shopify;

preserving important content and SEO pages;

transforming a content-driven website into a complete ecommerce storefront;

creating a new visual identity and interface;

building a clearer path from content discovery to product purchase;

implementing the new design directly in Shopify.

From a Content Website to a Complete Ecommerce Experience

The most important aspect of the project was the transformation itself.

The original Urban Sign Print website was primarily designed to provide information and content. Customers could learn about the business and its offerings, but the website did not function as a complete online store.

With the move to Shopify, we introduced the missing ecommerce layer.

The new storefront allows customers to:

browse the product catalog;

view product information;

select products for purchase;

add products to the shopping cart;

proceed through the purchasing flow;

complete orders through Shopify Checkout.

This changed the role of the website completely. Instead of functioning primarily as a digital information resource, it became a full ecommerce channel capable of supporting direct online sales.

The migration therefore became much more than a technical WordPress to Shopify migration. It was a complete ecommerce transformation built around the new commercial goals of the website.

Building a New Shopify Storefront Architecture

Moving from a content-focused WordPress structure to an ecommerce-focused Shopify storefront required more than simply adding product pages.

The existing content needed to work alongside a new product catalog, collections, cart, and purchasing journey. This meant reconsidering how information was structured and how users moved through the site.

We developed the new storefront around a more flexible Shopify architecture with:

custom page sections;

reusable content components;

redesigned content blocks;

product-focused layouts;

ecommerce-specific page elements;

responsive layouts for desktop, tablet, and mobile.

This created a Shopify reusable section architecture that allowed the new design to remain consistent while providing enough flexibility for different types of content.

Rather than treating every page as an isolated design, the storefront was built from reusable components that could support the broader structure of the new Shopify store.

Custom Shopify Design and Full Storefront Redesign

The migration was also used as an opportunity to completely rethink the visual presentation of Urban Sign Print.

We developed a new visual concept and redesigned the main pages of the website, including the structure and positioning of individual content blocks.

The redesign focused on creating a more modern and consistent interface while adapting the visual language of the original content website to a new ecommerce environment.

Our work included:

developing a new visual direction;

restructuring key pages;

redesigning content blocks;

improving visual hierarchy;

adapting layouts to product-oriented content;

creating a consistent user interface across the storefront.

This resulted in a custom Shopify storefront design rather than a simple migration of the existing WordPress appearance.

The new design was created specifically around the requirements of an online store, allowing content and ecommerce functionality to work together as one experience.

Creating a Clear Path From Content to Purchase

One of the key UX challenges was connecting the site's existing informational content with its new commercial functionality.

On a content-focused website, users primarily consume information. On an ecommerce website, that information should also help users move toward a product and, ultimately, a purchase.

We therefore reworked the user journey to make the transition between content discovery and shopping more natural.

The new experience provides a clearer path from:

content → product discovery → product selection → cart → checkout

This meant improving how products were presented and making key product information easier to access.

The result is a more structured Shopify ecommerce UX where informational content and commercial functionality support each other instead of operating as separate parts of the website.

Product Catalog and Ecommerce Functionality

Introducing a product catalog was a fundamental part of transforming Urban Sign Print into an ecommerce store.

The new Shopify implementation includes dedicated product presentation and the functionality required to support online purchasing.

We focused on creating product pages that provide the information customers need while fitting naturally into the redesigned storefront.

The ecommerce implementation included:

product catalog organization;

product presentation;

product information layouts;

add-to-cart functionality;

shopping cart functionality;

the complete path toward checkout.

This established the core infrastructure required for a Shopify online store and gave Urban Sign Print a direct sales channel that was not available in the original content-focused website.

Custom Shopify Development and Reusable Components

The new design was implemented directly in Shopify rather than being treated as a separate visual layer.

We developed the required Shopify sections and reusable components to reproduce the new visual system while keeping the storefront flexible for future content changes.

The development approach focused on:

reusable Shopify sections;

adaptable content components;

custom page layouts;

product-oriented storefront elements;

responsive behavior;

integration of the new design with Shopify's ecommerce functionality.

This gave the store a stronger Shopify custom theme development foundation and made the new interface easier to maintain as the business continues to expand its online presence.

Responsive Shopify Storefront Development

Because the new website needed to serve customers across different devices, the redesigned storefront was developed with responsive behavior from the beginning.

The interface was adapted for:

desktop;

tablet;

mobile.

This was particularly important for the new ecommerce journey. Product discovery, product information, cart interactions, and the transition toward checkout all needed to remain clear and usable regardless of screen size.

The result is a more consistent responsive Shopify ecommerce experience across the full customer journey.

UX Improvements Across the Shopping Journey

The redesign was not limited to visual changes. A major part of the project was improving how users interact with the website.

We simplified the way customers navigate the catalog and made important product information more accessible.

The UX improvements focused on:

easier product discovery;

clearer product presentation;

improved information hierarchy;

stronger connections between content and products;

a more understandable purchasing path;

smoother navigation from product discovery to checkout.

These changes helped turn the website into a more coherent Shopify customer journey, where the different stages of the experience — content, catalog, product selection, cart, and checkout — work together.

SEO-Focused WordPress to Shopify Migration

Because the existing website already contained valuable content and established SEO pages, preserving its search visibility was an important part of the migration.

Before moving the website, we reviewed the existing content structure and identified important pages that needed to be retained in the new Shopify implementation.

The migration process included:

analyzing the existing website structure;

preserving important SEO content and pages;

maintaining key content wherever possible;

configuring the necessary Shopify redirects;

checking important SEO elements after migration;

validating the accessibility and availability of key pages.

This allowed the redesign and ecommerce transformation to take place without treating SEO as an afterthought.

The goal was to create a new Shopify storefront while maintaining continuity between the existing WordPress website and its new ecommerce version.

Combining Migration, Redesign, and Ecommerce Development

What makes the Urban Sign Print project particularly significant is that several traditionally separate website projects were handled as one transformation.

The project combined:

WordPress to Shopify migration;

full visual redesign;

Shopify custom development;

ecommerce implementation;

UX restructuring;

product catalog creation;

cart and checkout implementation;

SEO migration.

Each part influenced the others.

The new design had to work with ecommerce functionality. The content structure had to work with product discovery. The UX had to support the purchasing journey. And the migration had to preserve important content and SEO pages while introducing an entirely new commercial layer.

This made the project a true Shopify ecommerce transformation, rather than a simple platform switch.

The Result: A New Shopify Ecommerce Channel

Urban Sign Print finished the project with more than a new platform and a redesigned interface.

The existing content-focused website was transformed into a complete Shopify ecommerce store with:

a product catalog;

product-focused pages;

shopping cart functionality;

online purchasing;

Shopify Checkout;

a completely redesigned storefront;

reusable Shopify sections;

responsive layouts;

an improved customer journey;

preserved SEO content and important pages.

The migration allowed Urban Sign Print to solve several business and technical challenges within a single project: move from WordPress to Shopify, modernize the entire visual experience, improve UX, and establish a new direct channel for online sales.

For Crocode, Urban Sign Print is a strong example of Shopify migration combined with full ecommerce redesign — turning an existing content website into a commercially capable online store while preserving valuable content and building a more modern, scalable user experience.

What Coffeeface Needed From Their Shopify Store

Coffeeface sits in a competitive beauty ecommerce brand space where visual identity matters, but clarity matters even more. Their product is unusual enough to need explanation. It is not a traditional moisturizer, cleanser, or serum. It is a portable, routine-simplifying skincare format that needs to be understood quickly if it is going to sell well.

That creates a common ecommerce challenge. If the storefront is too minimal, the product feels unclear. If it is too dense, the experience starts to feel like homework. Coffeeface needed a store that could explain the value of the product without overwhelming the customer.

The project focused on several priorities from the start. Better Shopify UX/UI redesign. Stronger Shopify product page optimization. Cleaner storytelling around ingredients and benefits. And a more natural shopping flow for a clean skincare brand built around convenience, portability, and daily use.

Designing Around Product Education and Simplicity

A skincare store like this has to do more than look premium. It has to help customers understand what makes the product different.

We structured the storefront around that idea. Instead of treating the product like a generic cosmetic item, we built the experience around its positioning: one-step skincare routine, portable skincare pads, and a faster way to handle daily skincare.

The design system emphasized:

cleaner hierarchy across homepage and product pages;

more focused messaging blocks;

stronger product-benefit presentation;

easier navigation on mobile;

visual balance between skincare education and purchase intent.

This kind of Shopify redesign for beauty ecommerce works best when the site does not try to say everything at once. We made sure the messaging moved in the right order: what it is, why it matters, how it works, and why it fits into everyday routines.

That structure is especially important for a travel-friendly skincare brand and minimal skincare routine product, where convenience is part of the value proposition.

Product Page Development for a Differentiated Skincare Offer

For Coffeeface, the product page was the most important sales environment on the site. That is where trust, explanation, and conversion all have to come together.

We approached the build with a strong Shopify PDP optimization focus. The product needed to feel simple to buy, but not oversimplified in how it was presented.

We refined the product page experience around several key goals:

clearer explanation of the 3-in-1 moisture pads concept;

stronger ingredient storytelling;

more persuasive benefit-led content blocks;

better sequencing of product details and usage guidance;

improved mobile readability and add-to-cart flow.

This was particularly important because Coffeeface’s positioning relies on both format and ingredients. It is not just about skincare outcomes — it is also about how the product fits into real life. Individually wrapped skincare pads, simplified routines, and easy travel use are all part of the buying logic, so the product page had to sell the system, not just the item.

Building a Storefront Around Ingredients and Benefits

Beauty and skincare customers often buy through ingredient trust as much as brand trust. That meant the storefront had to communicate not only what Coffeeface does, but why its formulation mattered.

We structured the content and design around the brand’s strongest skincare themes:

caffeine skincare

hydrating skincare products

antioxidant skincare

skin soothing skincare

daily hydration skincare

That kind of messaging is especially effective when the experience stays clean and digestible. We avoided turning the storefront into a technical ingredient encyclopedia. Instead, we focused on how the benefits are understood in a shopping context: depuffing, hydration, brightening, smoother-looking skin, and a more convenient daily routine.

This helped position Coffeeface more clearly as a premium skincare ecommerce brand rather than just another product in a crowded skincare feed.

Mobile UX for Beauty Ecommerce

A large share of skincare shopping happens on phones, especially for visually driven products discovered through social and paid traffic. That means mobile performance is not a secondary concern — it is the storefront.

We treated the mobile experience as a core part of the build rather than a compressed desktop version. Several design and UX decisions were made with mobile-first conversion in mind:

product sections that scan cleanly on smaller screens;

clearer touch-friendly purchase flow;

more compact ingredient and benefit presentation;

smoother visual rhythm between images, copy, and calls to action;

reduced friction in the add-to-cart journey.

For a Shopify beauty brand website, small UX decisions tend to have outsized impact. If the customer has to pinch, hunt, or decode what they are looking at, the sale usually disappears.

Our goal was to make Coffeeface feel polished, intuitive, and native to the way skincare shoppers already browse.

Conversion-Focused Shopify Development

From a development standpoint, this was not just about aesthetics. The store needed to support stronger Shopify conversion optimization and give the brand a better base for future growth.

We implemented a cleaner storefront structure with an emphasis on:

modular content flexibility;

stronger landing page flow;

better merchandising control;

improved product storytelling;

a more scalable Shopify frontend development foundation.

This gave Coffeeface a more usable and strategically organized Shopify setup — one that supports not only immediate product sales, but also future testing, iteration, and Shopify growth optimization.

That matters for beauty brands more than people often realize. Once paid traffic starts scaling, the storefront has to carry much more weight. Better design alone is not enough. The site has to hold attention, build trust, and close efficiently.

The Result: A More Focused Shopify Experience for a Beauty Brand

The finished Coffeeface store gave the brand a stronger digital foundation built around what makes the product distinctive. Rather than treating it like a generic skincare launch, we built a more intentional Shopify development for skincare ecommerce experience — one designed around routine simplification, product clarity, and conversion.

This project brought together several things that matter most in Shopify CRO for skincare brand work:

stronger visual positioning;

clearer product communication;

better mobile UX;

more persuasive product pages;

a more scalable ecommerce structure.

For Crocode, Coffeeface is a strong example of how Shopify development for a beauty brand works best when strategy and product positioning drive the build. The result is not just a better-looking store. It is a storefront that makes the product easier to understand, easier to trust, and easier to buy.

Migrating Multiple Bannerprinting Stores From WooCommerce to Shopify

Bannerprinting needed to move several active regional ecommerce stores from WooCommerce to Shopify without disrupting their existing content, product catalog, or commercial operations.

The challenge was not simply to move products from one platform to another. Each regional store had its own content and location-specific structure, while the migration also needed to preserve important URLs, maintain the existing catalog organization, and establish a reliable system for inventory and warehouse management on Shopify.

The project focused on:

migrating multiple active WooCommerce stores to Shopify;

preserving existing content, pages, articles, and media;

transferring the product catalog and adapting categories to Shopify Collections;

maintaining important URL structures for SEO;

configuring Shopify Locations and inventory management;

validating products, stock availability, links, redirects, and site structure before launch.

Planning a Multi-Store Shopify Migration

Moving a single ecommerce store between platforms is already a complex process. Migrating several regional stores adds another layer of complexity because the content, catalog, and structure need to remain consistent while still reflecting the requirements of each individual location.

For Bannerprinting, we approached the migration as a multi-store Shopify implementation rather than a simple data export and import.

The work covered several connected areas:

content migration;

product and catalog migration;

regional store adaptation;

inventory and warehouse configuration;

URL and redirect management;

final technical validation.

This allowed the stores to transition to Shopify while maintaining the core structure that existing customers and search engines were already familiar with.

Migrating Existing Content Without Losing the Site Structure

One of the main requirements was to preserve the existing content of the Bannerprinting stores.

We migrated the site's existing:

pages;

articles;

informational content;

images;

other required media assets.

The goal was not simply to reproduce the content on a new platform. The migration also needed to preserve the core content structure and important URLs wherever possible.

This is particularly important during a platform migration because changes to URLs and content organization can affect both user navigation and organic search performance.

As part of the process, we reviewed the resulting Shopify structure and configured the necessary Shopify redirects to help maintain continuity between the old and new versions of the stores.

WooCommerce Product Catalog Migration to Shopify

The product catalog was another major part of the migration.

We transferred the core product information from WooCommerce into Shopify, including:

product names;

product descriptions;

product images;

key product attributes;

catalog organization.

The existing WooCommerce categories also had to be adapted to the Shopify data model.

Instead of reproducing the category system exactly as it existed in WooCommerce, we mapped the catalog structure to Shopify Collections, creating an organization that works naturally within the new platform.

After the migration, we checked the product pages and catalog structure to make sure that the transferred information was displayed correctly and that products remained properly organized.

This created a more reliable foundation for ongoing Shopify product catalog management.

Adapting Multiple Regional Stores

Bannerprinting operated several regional stores, which meant the migration could not be treated as a single generic storefront deployment.

Each store had to retain its appropriate location-specific structure and content while moving to the same underlying ecommerce platform.

Our work included:

migrating the individual regional stores;

adapting each store to its corresponding location;

validating regional content;

checking product catalogs;

verifying that the resulting Shopify stores maintained their expected structure.

This approach allowed Bannerprinting to move multiple existing ecommerce operations to Shopify while preserving the distinctions between the regional storefronts.

Shopify Locations and Inventory Management

Inventory management was another important part of the migration.

The new Shopify setup required an appropriate structure for Locations, warehouses, and product availability. We configured the store's locations and established the necessary relationship between products, inventory, and available quantities.

The work included:

configuring Shopify Locations;

setting up inventory management;

checking product stock levels;

validating available quantities;

verifying the relationship between products and their corresponding locations.

This was particularly important because inventory availability needs to remain reliable after a platform migration. A product can be transferred successfully from a technical perspective while still producing incorrect availability information if the underlying location and inventory structure is not configured correctly.

For Bannerprinting, inventory validation therefore formed an important part of the overall migration process.

Technical Shopify Configuration After the Migration

Once the core content and catalog data had been transferred, we configured the essential Shopify store settings and reviewed the resulting site structure.

The technical work included:

configuring the main Shopify store settings;

checking page and collection structures;

setting up required redirects;

reviewing internal links;

validating the resulting URL structure;

checking the migrated catalog;

performing final pre-launch testing.

This stage was important because migration issues are not always visible immediately after data import. A product may exist but have incorrect availability. A page may be present but have the wrong URL. A collection may contain products but not reflect the intended catalog hierarchy.

The final validation process helped identify and resolve these issues before the stores went live on the new platform.

A Controlled Migration From WooCommerce to Shopify

The Bannerprinting project demonstrates that a successful WooCommerce to Shopify migration is about much more than transferring database records.

The migration had to account for several layers of an active ecommerce business at the same time:

content and media needed to remain available;

products and descriptions needed to transfer correctly;

categories needed to be adapted to Shopify Collections;

regional storefronts needed to retain their individual structure;

inventory and locations needed to work correctly;

URLs and redirects needed to be reviewed for continuity;

the complete storefront needed to be tested before launch.

By treating these areas as parts of one migration process, we were able to move the Bannerprinting stores to Shopify without rebuilding their ecommerce presence from the ground up.

The Result: A Unified Shopify Foundation for Multiple Stores

The Bannerprinting migration successfully moved several active regional WooCommerce ecommerce stores to Shopify, including their core content, product catalogs, media, and commercial infrastructure.

The resulting Shopify setup provides a unified platform for managing:

products and catalogs;

regional storefronts;

Shopify Collections;

locations and inventory;

content and media;

redirects and site structure.

For Crocode, Bannerprinting is a strong example of multi-store Shopify migration where the priority is not simply changing platforms, but preserving an existing ecommerce operation while creating a more manageable foundation for future growth.

The project combines WooCommerce to Shopify migration, catalog restructuring, regional store adaptation, Shopify inventory management, URL preservation, and technical validation into one controlled ecommerce migration process.

Migrating NEWROCK to Shopify With a New Ecommerce Navigation

NEWROCK needed more than a straightforward WordPress to Shopify migration. The existing website needed to retain its content and catalog while gaining the commercial infrastructure required for a reliable Shopify ecommerce experience.

At the same time, the existing navigation structure needed to be reconsidered. As the store's catalog and commercial functionality moved to Shopify, users needed a clearer way to find relevant categories, products, and sections.

The project therefore combined three core areas: Shopify migration, payment infrastructure, and navigation redesign.

From the beginning, the project centered on:

migrating the existing WordPress website to Shopify;

transferring products, catalog content, pages, and media;

configuring the required Shopify payment gateways;

validating the complete payment and checkout flow;

redesigning the site's main navigation and catalog structure;

improving product discovery and mobile navigation;

preserving SEO considerations while restructuring the site.

Migrating the Existing WordPress Website to Shopify

The first stage of the project was the migration of NEWROCK's existing website from WordPress to Shopify.

The goal was to move the existing website content into the new platform while adapting its structure to Shopify's ecommerce architecture.

The migration included:

existing website content;

product catalog;

product information;

pages and informational materials;

images;

required media assets.

The existing structure was then adapted to Shopify rather than simply reproduced as a one-to-one copy.

This allowed the new platform to support the requirements of a modern ecommerce store while retaining the content and product information that already existed on the WordPress website.

The result was a structured Shopify ecommerce foundation ready for the next stage of the project: payment configuration and a more effective navigation system.

Setting Up the Shopify Payment Infrastructure

A successful migration is not complete when products and pages appear correctly on the new platform. The commercial infrastructure also needs to work reliably.

For NEWROCK, we configured the required payment gateways in Shopify and verified the complete purchasing flow.

The payment implementation included:

connecting the required payment gateways;

configuring available payment methods;

checking the payment process;

running test transactions;

validating the Shopify Checkout flow after migration.

Testing was particularly important because payment functionality sits at the most critical point of the ecommerce journey.

A customer can browse the catalog and add products to a cart successfully, but the store still cannot function commercially if the payment and checkout process fails.

We therefore validated the complete flow from cart to Shopify Checkout, including test transactions before launch.

Redesigning the Main Store Navigation

One of the most important improvements in the project was the redesign of NEWROCK's navigation.

The existing structure was analyzed to identify ways to make the store easier to understand and reduce the effort required to reach important categories and products.

We redesigned the main navigation around a clearer catalog hierarchy, focusing on:

easier access to primary categories;

clearer organization of products;

improved category discovery;

more logical navigation between sections;

a structure compatible with Shopify Collections.

The objective was not simply to create a different menu. The navigation was redesigned as part of the broader Shopify information architecture.

This created a clearer relationship between the main menu, collections, category pages, and individual products.

Building a More Structured Shopify Collection System

The navigation redesign was closely connected to the structure of the catalog itself.

We configured the relevant Shopify Collections and associated pages so that users could move more naturally through the product hierarchy.

A well-structured collection system provides an important layer between the main navigation and individual product pages. It allows customers to first identify the category they need and then narrow their selection through the relevant products.

For NEWROCK, this meant creating a more coherent path:

main navigation → category → collection → product

This structure also provided a stronger foundation for future catalog growth because new products and categories can be incorporated into an established navigation system rather than added to an increasingly complex menu.

Improving Product Discovery and Ecommerce UX

The navigation changes were primarily driven by UX.

The goal was to reduce unnecessary steps between a customer's starting point and the products or categories they are looking for.

The improved structure focused on:

making major categories easier to find;

reducing navigation complexity;

improving product discovery;

creating clearer relationships between collections and products;

making the store easier to understand at a glance.

These changes created a more straightforward Shopify ecommerce UX and helped turn the site's navigation into an active part of the purchasing journey.

Instead of making users work through a complex structure to find relevant products, the new navigation provides a clearer route through the catalog.

Optimizing Navigation for Mobile Users

The navigation redesign also accounted for the way customers interact with ecommerce stores on smaller screens.

Mobile navigation needs to provide the same basic catalog logic as desktop navigation while working within a much more constrained interface.

We therefore optimized the structure for mobile users with a focus on:

easier access to important categories;

clearer navigation hierarchy;

simpler movement through the catalog;

improved product discovery on smaller screens.

This helped create a more consistent responsive Shopify navigation across desktop and mobile experiences.

The objective was not to create two separate information architectures, but to make the same underlying catalog structure easier to use across different devices.

SEO-Safe Navigation and URL Restructuring

Changing navigation and catalog structure can have consequences beyond UX.

When categories, collections, and pages are reorganized, URLs and internal links may also change. That means a navigation redesign needs to account for SEO from the beginning.

As part of the NEWROCK project, we reviewed the structural changes and handled the necessary technical SEO checks.

This included:

reviewing URL changes after migration;

configuring required Shopify redirects;

checking internal links after restructuring;

verifying access to important pages;

checking the availability of key pages for search engines.

This approach helped ensure that the new navigation could be improved without treating the existing SEO structure as something separate from the redesign.

The result was a more usable catalog structure with the necessary technical measures to support continuity after the migration.

Post-Migration QA Across the Ecommerce Journey

The final stage of the project was dedicated to validating the new Shopify implementation before launch.

The QA process covered the main areas affected by the migration and restructuring:

migrated pages;

product catalog;

collections;

navigation;

internal links;

redirects;

payment functionality;

Shopify Checkout;

mobile behavior;

accessibility of important pages.

Payment functionality received particular attention because it had to be tested independently from the content and navigation migration.

Test transactions helped verify that the configured payment infrastructure was working correctly before the store was released.

This created a more controlled transition from the original WordPress website to the new Shopify environment.

Combining Migration, Payments, and Information Architecture

What makes the NEWROCK project different from a standard platform migration is the combination of technical and UX work.

The project addressed three connected layers of the ecommerce experience:

Platform:
The existing WordPress website was migrated to Shopify with its products, content, pages, and media.

Commerce:
The required payment gateways were configured and the complete Shopify Checkout flow was tested.

Navigation:
The site's information architecture was redesigned around clearer categories, collections, product discovery, and mobile usability.

These areas work together.

A well-structured catalog is more useful when customers can actually find products. A good navigation system is more valuable when the checkout process works reliably. And a functional payment system is only useful if customers can efficiently reach the products they want to purchase.

This made the project a broader Shopify ecommerce implementation rather than a simple CMS migration.

